course: money
lesson: 22
title: The weekly shop and a family budget
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A budget is a plan for how much a family will spend; the weekly food shop is where that plan meets real life, one item at a time.
explain: Picture Saturday morning: Mum has £80 for the shop and a list of 20 things that adds up to £76, so there is £4 spare; Ella's £2 crisps just fit, but grab two more treats and the plan is broken.
explain: Families save money in clever, ordinary ways: a list, a meal plan, big bags of things that last, yellow-sticker bargains, packed lunches, and comparing shops.
explain: Shops are clever too: sweets sit by the tills where bored children wait, and shopping hungry makes everyone grab extra.
explain: When food prices go up, the same basket costs more, so a running total in your head or on a phone stops nasty surprises at the till.
fact: A budget is a plan for how much to spend; a shopping list helps a family buy only what it needs.
fact: A budget of £80 with a list adding up to £76 leaves £80 − £76 = £4 spare.
fact: A meal plan means deciding what to eat each day before shopping, so less food is thrown away; wasting £10 of food a week is £40 in four weeks.
fact: Buying in bulk means buying a lot at once: a 5kg bag of rice at £5 is cheaper than five 1kg bags at £1.50, which cost £7.50, so it saves £2.50.
fact: Bulk buying only saves money on things that last and get used, like rice and pasta, not on food that goes off.
fact: Reduced food has a yellow sticker because it should be eaten soon; bread cut from £1.20 to 30p saves 90p a loaf, so £1.80 on two.
fact: Shopping hungry leads to impulse buys, which are sudden wishes to buy; one hungry shop cost £90 instead of £70, which is £20 extra.
fact: Shops place sweets by the tills on purpose; 80p twice a week is £1.60 a week and £6.40 over four weeks.
fact: A running total means adding up the prices as you go; at £43 of a £50 budget, £7 is left, so an £8 cake does not fit.
fact: Different supermarkets charge different prices; a basket at £62 in one and £54 in another saves £8 a week, which is £416 over 52 weeks.
fact: A packed lunch costs about £1.50 instead of £4 for a bought lunch, saving £2.50 a day, or £12.50 over five school days.
fact: The cost of living is how much money people need for basic things; when food prices rise, a £70 shop can become £80, which is £40 more over four weeks.
exercise: What is a family budget? | A plan for how much the family will spend | A bag or purse that holds the family's money | A list of all the shops in town and their prices | A bill the supermarket sends to the family each week
exercise: Mum's budget is £80 and her list adds up to £76. How much is spare? | £4 | £6 | £14 | £156
exercise: Why does Dad plan the week's meals before shopping? | So he buys only what they will eat and less goes in the bin | So the supermarket gives him a special discount | So the food stays fresh for an extra month | So the family can skip breakfast to save money
exercise: Last month the family threw away about £10 of food a week. How much is that over 4 weeks? | £40 | £14 | £10 | £400
exercise: Small 1kg bags of rice cost £1.50. What do five of them cost? | £7.50 | £5.50 | £6.50 | £1.55
exercise: The 5kg bag costs £5 and five small bags cost £7.50. How much does the big bag save? | £2.50 | £5 | £1.50 | £12.50
exercise: Mum buys two loaves cut from £1.20 to 30p each. How much does she save in total? | £1.80 | 90p | 60p | £2.40
exercise: Why do shops put yellow stickers on bread late at night? | It needs eating soon, so they sell it cheaply | It is old bread that is not safe to eat at all | They are clearing shelves for a new kind of bread | Yellow means the bread has extra vitamins added
exercise: Dad shopped hungry and spent £90 instead of the usual £70. How much extra? | £20 | £16 | £160 | £30
exercise: Why are sweets placed right by the tills? | To tempt people who are waiting, especially children | Because the law says sweets must be kept near the door | Because the tills need sugar nearby to keep working properly | So the shop can keep them well out of the hot sunlight
exercise: Sweets at the till cost 80p, bought twice a week. How much is that in 4 weeks? | £6.40 | £3.20 | £1.60 | £8.40
exercise: Mum's budget is £50 and she is at £43. The cake is £8. Can she buy it? | No, only £7 is left | Yes, £8 is left over | Yes, £13 is left over | No, nothing at all is left
exercise: The same basket costs £62 at one shop and £54 at another. How much is saved in a week? | £8 | £12 | £116 | £6
exercise: Saving £8 a week, how much is saved in 52 weeks? | £416 | £400 | £360 | £60
exercise: A bought lunch is £4 and a packed lunch about £1.50. How much is saved over 5 school days? | £12.50 | £2.50 | £27.50 | £7.50
exercise: Mum's usual £70 shop now costs £80. How much more is that over 4 weeks? | £40 | £10 | £30 | £320
test: Ella goes, "It's only crisps, they don't count, they're not on the list." What's the best answer? | Everything in the trolley counts towards the £80 budget | Crisps are free if they are not on the list | Snacks are paid for by the shop, not the family | Only things on the list get added up at the till
test: Your mate, a proper Gen Alpha kid, says, "Packed lunch is mid, bought lunch is bussin'." ("Mid" means meh; "bussin'" means really tasty.) His mum says the packed lunch saves money. How much a week, if it's £1.50 instead of £4? | £12.50 | £2.50 | £5.50 | £20
test: What would happen if Mum bought a 5kg bag of fresh strawberries because "big bags are cheaper"? | Lots would go off before they were eaten, so money would be wasted | They would keep for months, so it's a clever saving | The shop would give her the strawberries for free | Strawberries never go off, so nothing would happen
test: Gran tuts, "Sweets by the tills — they do that on purpose, you know." Your cousin says, "Nah, that's just where they fit." Who is right? | Gran: shops place them where waiting children see them | Your cousin: it's just where there happened to be a spare shelf | Neither: sweets are always put at the back | Both: the law says sweets go by the tills
test: Dad, starving: "Just chuck it in the trolley, I'm well hank marvin'." ("Hank Marvin" is London rhyming slang for starving.) What usually happens on a hungry shop? | People buy more than they need, like £90 instead of £70 | People buy less, because hunger makes them tired | Nothing changes, the list decides everything | The shop gives hungry people a discount
test: A girl from Newcastle says, "Me mam's canny with money, she gets the yellow-sticker bread for 30p." ("Canny" means clever or good.) If it's normally £1.20, how much does she save on one loaf? | 90p | 30p | £1.50 | 80p
test: Ollie's dad says, "£8 a week ain't nothing, but it's not much either." Is £8 a week a big saving over a whole year? | Yes, it's £416 over 52 weeks | No, it's only £52 over a year | No, it's only £80 over a year | Yes, it's £8,000 over a year
test: Mum goes, "Right, we're at £43 — wait, no, I forgot the milk, £44." Her budget is £50. How much is left? | £6 | £7 | £4 | £94
test: In a cartoon, a family "saves money" by buying ten of everything and throwing half away. What's wrong? | Food thrown away is money wasted, not saved | Nothing, because buying more always saves money | Throwing food away makes the shop refund you | Food thrown in the bin is paid for by the council
test: Mum mutters, "Same stuff, ten quid more." The shop was £70 and is now £80. What does it mean for the family? | The cost of living has gone up, so they may swap brands or cut treats | The family is buying more food than last month | The shop has given them a £10 discount | Prices went down, so they can buy extra
test: Your big brother says, "Budgets are for skint people, rich families just buy whatever." What's the wisest reply? | Any family can use a budget to spend money well | He's right, only people with no money plan spending | Budgets are only for shops, never for ordinary families | Rich families pay nothing, so they don't need one
exam: Ascertain the residual sum available to a household which has allocated eighty pounds to its weekly provisions, whereas the items enumerated upon its list amount to seventy-six pounds. | £4 | £6 | £14 | £156
exam: It is given that one retailer charges £62 and another £54 for an identical assortment of goods. Determine the saving obtainable over a period of fifty-two weeks by patronising the latter. | £416 | £400 | £364 | £468
exam: Identify the principal reason for which confectionery is customarily displayed adjacent to the tills of a supermarket. | The inducement of unplanned purchases by customers, in particular children, who are obliged to wait | Compliance with a statutory requirement concerning the placement of confectionery | The protection of confectionery from deterioration caused by sunlight | The convenience of staff responsible for the replenishment of shelves
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the purchase of goods in bulk. | It is economical in respect of durable goods that will be consumed, such as rice and pasta | It is economical in respect of every product, including perishable fruit | It invariably results in a greater total expenditure than small purchases | It is prohibited in respect of households of fewer than five persons
